#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133407 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74196b81-0e52-5d10-a099-ccd849d03a98" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation Over Space Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "4f12e12d-37b3-5c41-9284-bc369ccd4c0f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12421" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = y \\,\\mathbf{i} -x \\,\\mathbf{j} + z\\,\\mathbf{k}$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t) =  \\sin (\\pi t)\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\cos (\\pi t)\\, \\mathbf{j} + t(2-t)\\,\\mathbf{k}$ for $t \\in [0,2].$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r\n= \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\sin (\\pi t), \\qquad\ny = \\cos (\\pi t), \\qquad\nz = t(2-t) = 2t-t^2.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i-x\\,j+z\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+(2t-{t}^{2})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2(1-t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+(2t-{t}^{2})\\,k\\,)\\cdot (\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2(1-t)\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))+(2t-{t}^{2})\\cdot 2(1-t) \\\\  & =\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+2(2t-{t}^{2})(1-t) \\\\  & =\\pi ({cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t))+2(2t-3{t}^{2}+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\pi +4t-6{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\pi +4t-6{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\pi t+2{t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[2\\pi +8-16+8]-[0] \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133408 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f0629bb-3a37-5ab8-8449-50789b295c7a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "87c9cf21-797c-5893-a81b-580685331739" :question/math-academy-id "e-12422" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} = \\langle -xz, \\: -yz, \\: x+z^2 \\rangle,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ and the plane $z=1.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n= \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ and the plane $z=1.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz = g(x,y) = 1.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-xz,\\,-yz,\\,{z}^{2}+x⟩{|}_{z=1} \\\\  & =⟨{{-x}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{1+x}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(-x)\\cdot 0-(-y)\\cdot 0+1+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+x)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNow, note that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=1$ of radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r \\sin\\theta, \\qquad\n\\textrm{d}A = r \\, \\textrm{d}r \\textrm{d}\\theta,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1+x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }r+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[r\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\pi r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\pi [{r}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133409 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d65a039f-6022-547d-a852-c249da5bd8ab" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Surface Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "f3f9694a-14f8-54c3-be32-744c2dd51736" :question/math-academy-id "e-12435" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t) = (t^2-t^3)\\,\\mathbf{i} + (t^2-t) \\, \\mathbf{j} + (t^2-t)\\,\\mathbf{k}$ for $t \\in [0,1).$\nGiven that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\mathbf{i} + x\\, \\mathbf{j} + y\\,\\mathbf{k},$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n= \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = t^2-t^3, \\qquad\ny = t^2-t, \\qquad\nz = t^2-t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =i+x\\,j+y\\,k \\\\  & =i+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,j+({t}^{2}-t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,k \\\\  & =(2t-3{t}^{2})\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j+(2t-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(i+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,j+({t}^{2}-t)\\,k)\\cdot ((2t-3{t}^{2})\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j+(2t-1)\\,k) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (2t-3{t}^{2})+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\cdot (2t-1)+({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (2t-1) \\\\  & =2t-3{t}^{2}+(-{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (2t-1) \\\\  & =2t-3{t}^{2}-2{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}+4{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-2{t}^{2}+t \\\\  & =3t-7{t}^{2}+5{t}^{3}-2{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t-7{t}^{2}+5{t}^{3}-2{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{7}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{4}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74196b81-0e52-5d10-a099-ccd849d03a98" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation Over Space Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2016fc5-d910-5d89-94c7-22086ba208ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130870} {:answer/id #uuid "f2b13363-d1b8-5235-ae4b-62a35c52b74f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130871} {:answer/id #uuid "2b92f96e-0dd9-52dd-bc0d-95d73e63bf87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130872} {:answer/id #uuid "6799356e-a75c-5973-93fa-b01eef60d7d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130873} {:answer/id #uuid "7e849629-c0dd-5d5b-baa4-07f7ad8794c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e849629-c0dd-5d5b-baa4-07f7ad8794c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130874} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b633639-0a85-5465-a9c5-7574739f0732" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "73fef5f2-e793-56dc-a75a-943258eb19b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-161925" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+(1-{t}^{2})\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=1-{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i-x\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j-2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j-2t\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+0\\cdot (-2t) \\\\  & =-\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)-\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-\\pi )\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\pi [t]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\pi (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134053 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f0629bb-3a37-5ab8-8449-50789b295c7a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94dd2b94-a8ff-53e1-9e70-4ec0db0c765c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\,20-4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371771} {:answer/id #uuid "598f20df-cb3c-538a-a358-7a96a145ff64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371772} {:answer/id #uuid "a2a944e0-6aa3-58b8-89d0-38f773be8c7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,20-4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371773} {:answer/id #uuid "6c11abc6-1a7d-5d8b-905e-67840eb74acf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371774} {:answer/id #uuid "e62ec9a9-a34a-5e8b-b887-7259b74efe21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,20-4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "598f20df-cb3c-538a-a358-7a96a145ff64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371772} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbf1a81a-2024-56dc-a9d2-5477498cb0c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d82e0bcb-c8b4-5aa2-9f3c-8a84246a499f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161927" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the paraboloid $z=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x+y=1,$ $x=0,$ and $y=0.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨-2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩{|}_{z=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨-2(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}),\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20)\\cdot (-2x)-0\\cdot (-2y)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071512/assets/c181e209cca01a1d6e372caa7f7810a5fa4aa9f177595bd92f937a7cba8b8a75.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371776 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74196b81-0e52-5d10-a099-ccd849d03a98" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation Over Space Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e953127-3e7f-5d3b-a6ce-63e196c30b13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371778} {:answer/id #uuid "49f2945c-9fd4-51f4-a73a-94501a4577e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371779} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad577fb-c677-56a1-a4ae-c57ff62e0b63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371780} {:answer/id #uuid "1874ae4f-0c2d-5dec-9721-25304717335b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371781} {:answer/id #uuid "37952105-255e-50cb-a598-7b9e80bc7842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37952105-255e-50cb-a598-7b9e80bc7842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371782} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f0f5f88-e1e1-5d82-8fbb-6267a5c189c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18b4792b-7a5f-5b55-a314-a72dcb643dc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162220" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i+x\\,j$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,z=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+0\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74196b81-0e52-5d10-a099-ccd849d03a98" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation Over Space Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1de08512-6801-5563-b8ba-d9c01a49b2dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371785} {:answer/id #uuid "49bd6b22-80ce-5742-9bac-4e4fcfa5b88c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371786} {:answer/id #uuid "8324520b-0247-5e37-bcfb-734d0fb617fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371787} {:answer/id #uuid "f857a715-2bca-5235-bb0c-40a5e7f256d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371788} {:answer/id #uuid "69a7c4a0-ff37-5702-8d75-1b3104e5f45a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69a7c4a0-ff37-5702-8d75-1b3104e5f45a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371789} :answer-field/id #uuid "dac1e85c-a82e-5911-938f-350967d20905" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b87f7a8c-ae78-5599-8dbd-eb9f9f74e11a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162230" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i-x\\,j+z\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k$ for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,z={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i-x\\,j+z\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j+2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j+2t\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+{t}^{2}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+2{t}^{3} \\\\  & =1+2{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(1+2{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[t+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =((\\pi -(-\\pi ))+\\frac{{\\pi }^{4}-(-\\pi )^{4}}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74196b81-0e52-5d10-a099-ccd849d03a98" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation Over Space Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a721f6a7-2a45-599f-b522-1c2c16fa21a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371792} {:answer/id #uuid "7f312e9f-5a51-5802-b9a3-6ce6217b45fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371793} {:answer/id #uuid "67949316-b40e-54cd-a6d5-527c161fbe19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371794} {:answer/id #uuid "22e0f445-10fd-54db-bd6d-3fc39a8a7365" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371795} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8edb90-a84d-5441-b6d5-df9994d1d83f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22e0f445-10fd-54db-bd6d-3fc39a8a7365" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371795} :answer-field/id #uuid "e00bb812-4c4c-5c7b-89e8-eb23edf81937" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b01e3497-8211-5b04-8ac1-5a1601603340" :question/math-academy-id "q-162235" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=(x+y)\\,i-z\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=(1-{t}^{2})\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=1-{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(x+y)\\,i-z\\,k \\\\  & =(1-{t}^{2}+{t}^{2})\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k \\\\  & =i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =-2t\\,i+2t\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k)\\cdot (-2t\\,i+2t\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2t)+0\\cdot 2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =-2t+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-2t+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-{t}^{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)}{4}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-1-\\frac{1}{4})-(-1-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371797 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74196b81-0e52-5d10-a099-ccd849d03a98" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation Over Space Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d63b481-f170-56a0-820c-4a798c6d2d4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371799} {:answer/id #uuid "992af6f2-2016-5088-a693-820c67938da1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371800} {:answer/id #uuid "1b512e94-29ed-5660-ad51-26a943c4c761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371801} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc32a96-c6e3-5f58-827d-8851dc2e93ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371802} {:answer/id #uuid "61ff6dbc-1f91-5352-9bde-bb960ddd68d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bc32a96-c6e3-5f58-827d-8851dc2e93ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371802} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fef9c55-a741-558f-b364-0e955bf747b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bce8a844-7cb9-59ef-8736-9bec28027032" :question/math-academy-id "q-162237" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=-z\\,j+y\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =-z\\,j+y\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k) \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t. \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[t]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371804 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d65a039f-6022-547d-a852-c249da5bd8ab" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Surface Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a20955b2-592a-5d77-ac37-661c0c2579d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371806} {:answer/id #uuid "6f6eff0f-b00d-5916-9907-7cbfe3b55b88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371807} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ccebc8-0dd6-5dcf-9cdb-4376f1e37982" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-5}{103}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371808} {:answer/id #uuid "58a2669b-e9b1-5a13-b821-0364b95f37a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371809} {:answer/id #uuid "ca68380b-6c84-53b6-91d4-75fa9ef26720" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca68380b-6c84-53b6-91d4-75fa9ef26720" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371810} :answer-field/id #uuid "392e017b-24f0-5244-bdc2-1da38e3e945b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b615b03d-f043-50ea-b480-60d64fb114c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-162316" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\,j+({t}^{3}-t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,0).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=z\\,i+y\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{3}-2t,\\,y=3{t}^{2}+3t,\\,z={t}^{3}-t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =z\\,i+x\\,k \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-t)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\,j-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-t)\\,k \\\\  & =(6{t}^{2}-2)\\,i+(6t+3)\\,j+(3{t}^{2}-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(({t}^{3}-t)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\,k)\\cdot ((6{t}^{2}-2)\\,i+(6t+3)\\,j+(3{t}^{2}-1)\\,k) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-t)\\cdot (6{t}^{2}-2)+0\\cdot (6t+3)+(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =6{t}^{5}+9{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(6{t}^{5}+9{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{6}+\\frac{9{t}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}-{t}^{3}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(1-\\frac{9}{5}+\\frac{1}{4}+1-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d65a039f-6022-547d-a852-c249da5bd8ab" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Surface Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1202d2aa-d66a-5634-bd2e-399ef6d4bfaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371813} {:answer/id #uuid "5adb85d2-3563-531e-a5d8-04aa47e96da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371814} {:answer/id #uuid "fdcb8f6d-46bb-5c3f-a4f7-0f2edaea6bba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371815} {:answer/id #uuid "aca4c20d-00de-5eb2-9dee-e18b66fe96e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371816} {:answer/id #uuid "e785b891-0cc0-5762-b698-bbf3c12a4a0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1202d2aa-d66a-5634-bd2e-399ef6d4bfaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371813} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad0e1b0b-7d53-5d7f-96ac-3cb8010d14e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "83c7df1b-5697-5938-b68a-ced77e0abb75" :question/math-academy-id "q-162324" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i+x\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))+0\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)) \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)-\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)-\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)}{2}+\\frac{\\pi t}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)}{4}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-[0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0-(0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0)] \\\\  & =-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f0629bb-3a37-5ab8-8449-50789b295c7a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccac1a52-dae7-52bc-8e8d-a0e3b920cb47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x-1}\\,-2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371820} {:answer/id #uuid "798f0abe-a725-5062-b5be-8ef234f1f6a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{0}\\,-2xy-1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371821} {:answer/id #uuid "254fe2f5-7961-5582-9b8c-cca7845d3393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{0}\\,2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371822} {:answer/id #uuid "8568b9ab-fd3a-5b35-a9fe-de9fab20dd3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x-1}\\,2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371823} {:answer/id #uuid "46edb570-a24b-5665-86c9-a871c103b4b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "254fe2f5-7961-5582-9b8c-cca7845d3393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{0}\\,2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371822} :answer-field/id #uuid "011244fc-c455-5385-82b4-0139d9eb50db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ce55f8d2-cf70-5f6b-9799-c8329a828edd" :question/math-academy-id "q-162361" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the paraboloid $z=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x-y=1,$ $x=0,$ and $y=0.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨y,\\,-x,\\,1⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨y,\\,-x,\\,1⟩{|}_{z=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨y,\\,-x,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-x}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (2x)-(-x)\\cdot (4y)+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+1)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071512/assets/d84b3ecc79c58a128d2f7ea262692d9f66dd9882ff244fb7c3dadd2b2571b550.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2xy+1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{0}\\,2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f0629bb-3a37-5ab8-8449-50789b295c7a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3b25243-c59a-550f-b605-ad3ab80eb2ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{4}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371827} {:answer/id #uuid "4aa0df3c-f83a-5f42-ad7d-90689fc29c8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{xy}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371828} {:answer/id #uuid "e17188e9-5fba-5d0b-9b94-2c1a191777ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371829} {:answer/id #uuid "f02ff5e5-ca4c-5a56-8bad-1d1e6d9e90f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371830} {:answer/id #uuid "bea4d31a-4afa-586b-9a6d-c8611ac2c59c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f02ff5e5-ca4c-5a56-8bad-1d1e6d9e90f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371830} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c71239c-7d99-5ad4-8fb5-edd3a2da2028" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a28026ac-9427-5db7-b40f-31513274adc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162365" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x=\\pm 1,$ and $y=\\pm 1.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨x,\\,y,\\,-2z⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the surface $z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our surface $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})=\\frac{-2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})=\\frac{-2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,-2z⟩{|}_{z=1/({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =⟨{{x}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x\\cdot \\frac{-2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}-y\\cdot \\frac{-2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071512/assets/b215ec1e3330f96e7e606e085721b2dd1f50d113f0151e36ec98e6d0a6f54e73.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f0629bb-3a37-5ab8-8449-50789b295c7a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a755254-cbb1-55b4-a9e7-3ff3d1325295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371834} {:answer/id #uuid "d4df7d4c-9880-5e86-9643-4552156e5f7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371835} {:answer/id #uuid "05dc34f6-6898-555b-81d0-0e41b42f4706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\pi }^{2}+{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371836} {:answer/id #uuid "9402e557-2610-5d81-9053-c251ece9bbb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371837} {:answer/id #uuid "26dcc658-d2d5-5c3a-a1f0-ede68a2bc1c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi +{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4df7d4c-9880-5e86-9643-4552156e5f7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371835} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8dd5e52-5957-522b-ad4d-1d2e44e1233a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "71240cf4-ff1e-5588-9e08-c1a6960656ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-162366" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨-x{e}^{z},\\,0,\\,{e}^{z}⟩,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=4.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=4.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=4.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-x{e}^{z},\\,0,\\,{e}^{z}⟩{|}_{z=4} \\\\  & =⟨{{-x{e}^{4}}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{{e}^{4}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-x{e}^{4})-0\\cdot 0+{e}^{4})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{4}\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ with radius $2$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{4}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{4}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={e}^{4}[\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{r=0}^{r=2} \\\\  & ={e}^{4}(2\\pi -0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =4\\pi {e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371839 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f0629bb-3a37-5ab8-8449-50789b295c7a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3936ab3-fd78-5a0c-9502-716208758a6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371841} {:answer/id #uuid "b19e3be0-6c62-5d54-a527-efa5f9bf650a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371842} {:answer/id #uuid "74ec3564-4df4-511d-a8ed-fb4c4906b434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371843} {:answer/id #uuid "d98bf2a3-de72-524c-be29-dbc1d61d1785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371844} {:answer/id #uuid "339943d1-ae30-5344-8633-7f28a1fd9e85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "339943d1-ae30-5344-8633-7f28a1fd9e85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371845} :answer-field/id #uuid "52ca2a53-216a-5d66-978d-06185103db1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0b752e92-877b-50d8-8a25-48e26cc2bae4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162374" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨0,\\,z,\\,x⟩,$ and $C$  is the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=x.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=x.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=x.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨0,\\,z,\\,x⟩{|}_{z=x} \\\\  & =⟨{{0}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{x}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{x}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot 1-x\\cdot 0+x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371846 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d65a039f-6022-547d-a852-c249da5bd8ab" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Surface Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c1d1a2f-4a39-5cd3-998a-4dc0c318780f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371848} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0498f5-15fc-5422-b1c8-dc30c83807e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371849} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f28afa-a7aa-547a-80e2-169d78862eed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371850} {:answer/id #uuid "83f03caf-15c0-5a56-ad24-86a4ae94b43a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371851} {:answer/id #uuid "d6485b49-3505-502c-bcc0-01907af31161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c1d1a2f-4a39-5cd3-998a-4dc0c318780f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371848} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9b6d44a-d252-5ddd-b45f-439a8bc232c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5d1ee8e-4914-56f3-ba74-1d7dcbbbad4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162886" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=({t}^{2}+t)\\,i+({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,j+(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,0).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i+z\\,j+x\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+t,\\,y={t}^{3}+{t}^{2},\\,z=2{t}^{2}+2t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+z\\,j+x\\,k \\\\  & =({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,i+(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\,j+({t}^{2}+t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\,k \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\,j+(4t+2)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,i+(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\,j+({t}^{2}+t)\\,k)\\cdot ((2t+1)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\,j+(4t+2)\\,k) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})(2t+1)+(2{t}^{2}+2t)(3{t}^{2}+2t)+({t}^{2}+t)(4t+2) \\\\  & =2t+11{t}^{2}+17{t}^{3}+8{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(2t+11{t}^{2}+17{t}^{3}+8{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{2}+\\frac{11}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{17}{4}{t}^{4}+\\frac{8}{5}{t}^{5}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(1-\\frac{11}{3}+\\frac{17}{4}-\\frac{8}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d65a039f-6022-547d-a852-c249da5bd8ab" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Surface Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3409134d-f4c2-5b85-98cb-3af488eaedf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371855} {:answer/id #uuid "e1cda993-9974-5ab3-90a0-0c8b89d7246c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371856} {:answer/id #uuid "35a92b56-0083-539e-9be2-0088db10460a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371857} {:answer/id #uuid "b53c8731-862b-5a5f-9c95-e52502994c20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371858} {:answer/id #uuid "a43dfcc2-9efa-51f5-8c9d-3e42f84da175" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b53c8731-862b-5a5f-9c95-e52502994c20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371858} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d3f772f-3f69-55dc-b492-c14492310703" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30e6116a-b26d-593c-9872-1c66e5adff35" :question/math-academy-id "q-162887" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=({t}^{3}-t)\\,i+(2{t}^{2}+1)\\,j+({t}^{2}-1)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i+3\\,j+yz\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-t,\\,y=2{t}^{2}+1,\\,z={t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+3\\,j+yz\\,k \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}+1)\\,i+3\\,j+(2{t}^{2}+1)({t}^{2}-1)\\,k \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}+1)\\,i+3\\,j+(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\,k \\\\  & =(3{t}^{2}-1)\\,i+4t\\,j+2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((2{t}^{2}+1)\\,i+3\\,j+(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}-1)\\,k)\\cdot ((3{t}^{2}-1)\\,i+4t\\,j+2t\\,k) \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}+1)(3{t}^{2}-1)+3(4t)+(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}-1)(2t) \\\\  & =-1+10t+{t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}+6{t}^{4}+4{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-1+10t+{t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}+6{t}^{4}+4{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-t+5{t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}+\\frac{6}{5}{t}^{5}+\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{6}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-1+5+\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{2}{3})-(1+5-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{12}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d65a039f-6022-547d-a852-c249da5bd8ab" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Surface Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b98cb58-c9c7-5bbe-9dc7-c06d25ae7ba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371862} {:answer/id #uuid "6ffb8641-cad8-567b-8a7c-8619e85947cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371863} {:answer/id #uuid "3523c6a2-fdd6-5d05-8aa6-574aa8f39240" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371864} {:answer/id #uuid "400d3580-8833-5322-a660-4e267f74e126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371865} {:answer/id #uuid "acff9520-6bb3-5ad8-af70-7f06439eeba8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b98cb58-c9c7-5bbe-9dc7-c06d25ae7ba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371862} :answer-field/id #uuid "daa2ac0c-c077-54a3-b146-fbd270744bd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "af282dee-c3f6-5e5f-85cc-c9fa9d489fdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-162892" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=2y\\,i-2x\\,j+{e}^{z-2y}\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2y\\,i-2x\\,j+{e}^{z-2y}\\,k \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)}\\,k \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+k)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+1\\cdot 2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =-2\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)-2\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =-2\\pi +2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-2\\pi +2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[-2\\pi t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-2\\pi (1)+0)-(-2\\pi (-1)+0) \\\\  & =-4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
